ls stubs each need two more members if the members are required.\n\n## 3. Rules a store cannot hold as written\nR1. Eight envelopes against RECORD_BYTES of 2 MiB. One envelope may be close to 2 MiB (custody.ts:340), so `#write` would throw too-large after a new signing already exists. I propose that before signing, custody checks the record plus the new envelope against the bound. If it does not fit, it refuses `full`, reason `bytes`, and signs nothing. That adds a reason beside `signings`. Please rule.\nR2. An archived refused record. S2 and S3 archive terminal records past 1,024 or 30 days. `read` still finds an archived record, so settle works. `byTarget` reads only live records. I propose accepting that: a signing dies within 960 s. Keeping refused records out of the archive would make the live set unbounded, because refused records do not count in the 256.\nR3. The 30 s lock of section 8. No store bounds a lock in time. S2 breaks a lock only when its holder has died. S1 returns null on contention. S3 queues the caller. C1 does not need the bound and changes no lock. NF-5's browser store should use navigator.locks.\nR4. Atomicity. Each version 2 write stays one whole write: a rename in S2, a transaction in S3. The `mismatch` rule of 5.3 settles the earlier envelopes \"in the same call\". That is one lock section with several network reads, not one write. Each answer is kept as its own outcome, so the next settle continues after a crash.\nR5. The browser store (NF-5). localStorage holds about 5 MiB, under three records at the bound, and has no atomic update across tabs. NF-5 needs IndexedDB, navigator.locks, and BroadcastChannel or the storage event.\n\n## 4. Conflicts between the note and custody.ts\n1. `settle` returns at once for every terminal phase (:423, :426). C1 adds a second predicate that excludes refused. TERMINAL stays for capacity, archival, S3's SQL and metrics.ts:11.\n2. `send` takes no read function (:369), and the mismatch rule of 5.3 needs one. 